```tsx
// App.tsx (example wiring, not required)
import React from 'react';
import AccountSettingsScreen from './AccountSettingsScreen';

export default function App() {
  return (
    <AccountSettingsScreen
      user={{
        name: 'Jordan Rivera',
        email: 'jordan@example.com',
        avatarUrl: undefined,
      }}
      onEditProfile={() => console.log('navigate to edit profile')}
      onNavigate={(routeKey) => console.log('navigate to', routeKey)}
      onDeleteAccount={async () => {
        // call your API here; throwing will surface an error alert
        await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1500));
      }}
      onLogout={() => console.log('log out')}
    />
  );
}
```

Notes on the implementation:
- **Profile row** shows avatar (or initials fallback) + name/email, tappable to edit profile.
- **Settings rows** are grouped into cards (Preferences, Security, Support) with dividers and optional trailing value text, matching native iOS/Android list conventions.
- **Delete account** uses `Alert.alert` with a `destructive` button style (red on iOS; Android shows the standard two-button dialog) so confirmation uses each platform's native pattern rather than a custom modal. The row disables itself and shows a spinner while the delete call is in flight, and surfaces a retry-able alert on failure.
- Styling uses `Platform.select` for shadow (iOS) vs. elevation (Android) on the card groups.
- No external dependencies — only `react-native` core components — so it works in any RN 0.7x project on both platforms.
